--- 2. THE DIGIT GUARD vs os.CreateTemp's UNDOCUMENTED FORMAT --- PASS, with a
        recorded residual weakness

  ANSWER, PLAINLY: YES. This does trade a string coupling between two packages
  for a string coupling to a standard-library implementation detail. That is a
  real and correct characterisation, and I confirmed the mechanism directly.

  The format is NOT an API contract. $GOROOT/src/os/tempfile.go:23:
      func nextRandom() string {
          return strconv.FormatUint(uint64(uint32(runtime_rand())), 10)
      }
  Base 10 lives here and nowhere else. The CreateTemp doc comment
  (tempfile.go:26-34) promises only "adding a random string to the end" — it
  says nothing about the alphabet, base or length. go1.26.6 linux/amd64.

  I probed IsStagingName (verbatim copy, storage.go:409-431) against plausible
  future formats:
      go1.26 actual   accounts.json.tmp-3787632045       -> true
      decimal (today) accounts.json.tmp-2496562633       -> true
      9-digit (<=1.22) accounts.json.tmp-483920117       -> true
      lowercase hex   accounts.json.tmp-9f3a1c2e         -> FALSE
      uppercase hex   accounts.json.tmp-9F3A1C2E         -> FALSE
      base36          accounts.json.tmp-1z9k4qx          -> FALSE
      base64url       accounts.json.tmp-Ab3-_x9Z         -> FALSE
      uuid-ish        accounts.json.tmp-3f2a1b4c-9d8e    -> FALSE
  So a format change WOULD stop IsStagingName matching. The coupling is real.

  BUT THE FAILURE MODE IS THE OPPOSITE ONE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
  Attempt 2's coupling failed SILENTLY — no test in the tree could observe it.
  This coupling fails LOUDLY, because the test derives its fixture from a REAL
  os.CreateTemp call, so producer and matcher are compared against each other
  on every single test run.

  I proved that rather than asserting it, with an isomorphic mutation: I
  inverted the guard to accept only NON-decimal suffixes, which is exactly what
  a future Go emitting hex/base36 would do to the current guard. Result:
    --- FAIL: TestSkipPredicate_StagingNames
        snapshot_test.go:193: SkipPredicate did not skip
        "sidecar.json.tmp-3807164669", a name produced by the real
        atomicWrite/createExclusive staging path
  A Go upgrade that changed the format would break the BUILD-TIME test at the
  same instant it broke production behaviour. It cannot regress unobserved.

  RESIDUAL WEAKNESS, recorded honestly and NOT a blocker: the tripwire is
  single-sample, hence probabilistic rather than deterministic. The test draws
  ONE random name. If a future format were 8-char lowercase hex, an all-decimal
  draw has probability (10/16)^8 = 2.3%, so the test would spuriously pass
  about 1 run in 43. (For 16-char hex it is 0.05%; for base36, ~0.005%.) So it
  is a ~97.7%-reliable loud tripwire, not a guarantee. I verified the
  accidental-pass case is real: IsStagingName("accounts.json.tmp-12345678")
  -> true, i.e. an all-digit hex draw would slip through.

  SHOULD IT BE CLOSED DIFFERENTLY? My judgement: acceptable as built, because
  it is tripwired, and the brief required only that the fixture be derived from
  the real staging path (it is). The cheapest deterministic close, if the lead
  wants one, is to loop the derivation in newStagingName (e.g. 100 draws, assert
  IsStagingName on each) — that makes detection certain at negligible cost and
  needs no design change. The alternative the lead floated — matching the
  separator without constraining the suffix — is strictly MORE robust against a
  stdlib change but over-matches legitimate data; see criterion 6, where I show
  that trade is currently free either way for real budget2 files. I do not
  recommend deriving the pattern from a live CreateTemp call at RUNTIME: that
  puts filesystem I/O into a pure predicate consulted per-file during a walk.

