--- 6. report.tmp-notes.csv NOT SKIPPED — RESOLUTION JUDGED CORRECT --- PASS

  snapshot_test.go:209-213 asserts skip("report.tmp-notes.csv") is false;
  enforced by the digit guard at storage.go:421-427.

  MY JUDGEMENT: the resolution is RIGHT, and I checked both directions of
  misclassification against the actual file namespace rather than reasoning
  abstractly. Being excluded from a backup is a SILENT, PERMANENT data loss in
  a money-data store, whereas retaining one stale staging file is cosmetic. The
  asymmetry favours under-skipping, which is what the guard does.

  CAN ANY REAL budget2 FILE BE MISCLASSIFIED? No, in either direction.
  - Internal writers use nine fixed names: accounts.json, aliases.json,
    amazon_enrichment.json, deleted_major_expenses.json, duplicate_decisions.json,
    major_expenses.json, transaction_pins.json, transfer_decisions.json, and
    imported *.csv. None contains ".tmp".
  - The ONLY user-controlled path into the data dir is the explorer upload,
    internal/handlers/explorer/handlers.go:911, which hard-rejects anything not
    ending ".csv": `if !strings.HasSuffix(strings.ToLower(filename), ".csv")`.
    So a user file can never end in ".tmp" (legacy branch unreachable), and a
    name like "budget.tmp-2024.csv" has non-digits after the separator, so the
    current branch does not match it either.
  The only name that could be wrongly skipped is a literal
  "<something>.tmp-<all digits>" with NO extension, which no writer produces
  and the upload path forbids. The guard costs nothing today.

