--- 8. restore.go PRUNE PROTECTION WORKS END TO END --- PASS

  Not a predicate unit test. I wrote my own test in a scratch copy OUTSIDE the
  repo driving the real Service.FromZip, and deleted the scratch tree after.
  restore.go:119 builds the predicate; restore.go:311 is where pruneExtras
  consults it for files.

  Setup: planted a REAL staging file via os.CreateTemp(dir,
  "accounts.json"+storage.StagingSuffix+"*"), plus a legacy "accounts.json.tmp",
  plus "stale.csv" and "report.tmp-notes.csv"; restored an archive containing
  only "fresh.csv" (so everything planted is absent from the archive).

    planted staging orphan: accounts.json.tmp-1994759186
    Result: Restored=1 Pruned=2 PruneFailures=0 SkippedProtected=0
    --- PASS

  Interpretation: the staging orphan and the legacy orphan BOTH survived on
  disk; Pruned=2 accounts for stale.csv and report.tmp-notes.csv, which proves
  pruning genuinely ran and the survival is protection rather than a no-op.
  That control matters — without it the test would pass vacuously. Confirmed
  load-bearing: under MUT-2 the same test reports Pruned=3 and
  "PRUNE PROTECTION BROKEN: staging orphan accounts.json.tmp-3021510352 was
  deleted by restore pruning".

